Development Meeting Summary

Date: 28 January 2026
Location / Platform: Main Library

1. Progress Update

The group has now held three meetings in total.

Core system architecture and responsibilities are clear and stable.

Current work has moved from high-level design to concrete implementation details, especially around data collection and indexing.

2. Data Collection and Storage Design

The crawler will periodically fetch news data using RSS feeds (tentatively every ~10 minutes).

A time-based database structure will be used to improve efficiency and freshness of search.

For each news item, we will initially store only essential metadata:

Headline

URL

Timestamp

Other key features if needed

Data fetched by the crawler will be:

Stored in the database for persistence

Exported as JSON / RSS-like objects to be passed to the indexer

Update Policy

If a fetched URL already exists in the database:

This indicates that the news content has been updated.

The old version will be ignored and replaced with the new one.

The system should allow retrieval of older news items when needed (e.g. historical search).

3. Optional Enhancements (Time Permitting)

Generate a short abstract or summary for each news item based on collected content.

This feature is optional and will only be implemented if time allows after core functionality is complete.

4. Week 3 Task Breakdown
ID	Task	Description	Depends on	Assignee
1	Finding a dataset	Identify a dataset containing news + metadata from previous years up to present	–	Xin
2	Crawler	Fetch news via RSS, store metadata in database, and output JSON objects for the indexer	Task 1	Leyan, Xin
3	IR – Boolean Search	Support AND / OR / NOT, phrase search, and proximity search with unlimited combinations	–	Aidan
4	IR – Free-text Search	Choose between TF-IDF or BM25, justify the choice, and implement it	–	Haya
5. IR and Query Support Decisions

Boolean search will support:

AND, OR, NOT

Phrase queries

Proximity search

Arbitrary combinations of operations (not limited to two tokens)

Example:
t1 AND t2 OR t3 AND NOT t4

Free-text search:

TF-IDF or BM25 will be selected based on effectiveness and justification.

The system should support queries of any length.

6. Frontend Considerations

To reduce user errors and improve usability:

Two UI modes will be implemented:

Boolean search mode

Free-text search mode

In boolean mode:

Users add query fields via a "+" button.

Operators (AND / OR / NOT / proximity) are selected from dropdown menus.

This avoids forcing users to manually learn query syntax.

7. Next Steps

Finalize and stabilize the crawler and database interaction.

Provide a basic but functional indexer implementation.

Begin integrating IR components with the frontend.

Prepare for evaluation once retrieval components are in place.

